\section{Introduction}
Anmatyerr (also Anmatjirra, Anmatjera; ISO 639-3 amx) is a Pama-Nyungan language of Central Australia, spoken in communities such as Ti Tree, Laramba and Mount Allan, roughly 200 km north of Alice Springs. AIATSIS records a Central and an Eastern dialect. Speaker estimates in the sources found vary (a publisher blurb mentions about 2,000 daily speakers). This report is based on a limited web search and is short on purpose.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
Search results describe the \emph{Central Anmatyerr Picture Dictionary} (Jenny Green, IAD Press, 2003; about 400 illustrations, Anmatyerr and English definitions), which its publisher presents as a resource for school children and teachers. No direct product URL for it appeared in the search results. No school curriculum or primer was found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Central \& Eastern Anmatyerr to English Dictionary (Jenny Green, IAD Press, 2010): \url{https://www.iadpress.com.au/products/central-eastern-anmatyere-to-eng-dictionary}
\item AIATSIS Austlang entry C8.1 (Anmatyerr), with bibliography: \url{https://collection.aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/c8.1}
\item AustLit record with publisher description: \url{https://www.austlit.edu.au/austlit/page/C790542}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None were found in the search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
The Endangered Languages Project lists a sample page for Anmatyerr: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/3840/samples/6882}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Printed dictionaries and a picture dictionary exist and could seed vocabulary and early literacy work, but no teaching programme or curriculum was found. Further enquiry with IAD Press and local communities would be needed.
\end{document}
